{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant during a turbulent period. Raymond inherited the county from his father Raymond II in 1154 facing immediate challenges from Muslim forces and internal political struggles. His reign was marked by constant warfare diplomacy and efforts to consolidate his power.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Maria of Antioch. He received a thorough education and was groomed for leadership. Upon his father's death Raymond ascended to the county at a young age inheriting a territory beset by enemies. He quickly demonstrated military prowess and political acumen to secure his position. His early years focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and maintaining alliances with neighboring rulers. He faced challenges from the Zengid dynasty a powerful Muslim force seeking to expand its influence in the region.\n\nRaymond III engaged in numerous military campaigns against the Zengids and other Muslim rulers. He fought to maintain Tripoli's independence and protect its Christian population. He allied with various Crusader states including the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch to counter Muslim aggression. His military leadership was crucial in repelling several Zengid offensives. He also invested in the development of Tripoli's infrastructure and economy fostering trade and prosperity.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat who understood the importance of alliances in maintaining power. He forged strategic partnerships with other Crusader states and European powers. He sought to balance his relationships with both Christian and Muslim rulers. He engaged in negotiations with Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Syria seeking to secure a lasting peace. While these negotiations were not always successful they helped to prevent large-scale conflicts. He also maintained close ties with the Papacy seeking papal support for his cause. His diplomatic efforts were essential in navigating the complex political landscape of the Levant.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III's reign was a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended the county from numerous attacks and fostered economic growth. He left behind a well-defended territory and a strong administrative system. He died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a capable military leader a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated defender of the Christian cause in the Levant. His reign represents a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli. He is often portrayed as a pragmatic ruler willing to negotiate with his enemies when necessary while remaining steadfast in his commitment to Christian values. His efforts to balance diplomacy and military strength helped to preserve Tripoli's independence for several decades.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0017849955474957824}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2533552050590515}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.2717786729335785}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.06198554113507271}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00024530262453481555}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.539125671319198e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.152531907195225e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.9457496313843876e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.003321608528494835}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -